00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:11.670
So one of the
fundamental challenges

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:15.500
is being able to combine these
data sets for the problem.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.040
It's not usually just
one data set gives us

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:19.300
the straight answer.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:21.960
And I think you're going to
see that from both of us today.

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:30.990
And so when I say satellites,
most people will think--

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:33.000
and with satellites
tracking ships,

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:36.870
I would guess a lot of you might
think this beautiful picture

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:38.280
of a ship on the ocean.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:40.920
And we look at this in
red, green, blue, kind

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:47.150
of the left column or the
left part of this diagram.

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:49.530
But often, there's
a problem with this.

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:51.590
There's a pesky
thing called clouds,

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:54.590
and I'm not talking
about cloud computing.

00:06:54.590 --> 00:07:00.290
It's clouds getting in between
the satellite and the Earth.

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:02.690
And so those can
abstract our view.

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:05.570
So sometimes it works,
and sometimes we

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:07.310
get really good
insights from that.

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:10.170
But sometimes we have to look
at other types of sensors.

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:12.780
So if we move to the
middle of this diagram,

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:15.500
we see another type of sensor
called synthetic aperture

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:16.790
radar.

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:21.280
And an example of this is from
the European Space Agency.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:25.570
We have a satellite
called Sentinel-1.

00:07:25.570 --> 00:07:27.830
What this does is it
sends a radar signal.

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:30.700
The satellite itself sends a
radar signal off the Earth.

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:32.590
It bounces off the Earth.

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:37.160
And then the satellite receives
that signal on another orbit.

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:39.070
This is great because
it penetrates right

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:40.000
through clouds.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:46.150
So that's problem solved, except
the refresh rates aren't always

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:49.030
really helpful when you're
tracking global supply

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:51.310
chains like shipping.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:53.270
And so that brings us
to this third data set.

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:54.670
And that's AIS.

00:07:54.670 --> 00:08:00.490
And so these ships are
sending a message or a ping

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:02.770
to a ground station,
and the satellites

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:06.610
are able to see and locate where
those radio frequencies were

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:08.520
transmitted from.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:11.120
So lots of pictures
in this talk.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:13.480
I hope you're already-- and
that's fine with everyone.

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:18.270
It's GIS, so it's very
visual crowd here.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:20.340
On the left, we
would have the types

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:24.750
of the different
bands of light that we

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:28.290
would use for modeling
agricultural crops.

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:30.960
So you can see the
bright green and purple

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:33.570
is showing basically
photosynthesis

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:35.940
and where it's occurring
in the crop health.

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:40.530
So we're able to build models
that kind of predict the yield.

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:44.010
When we move over right one and
we see that synthetic aperture

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:46.950
radar, what you're actually
seeing the differentiation

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:49.830
there is the fields
that have been recently

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:52.300
plowed and slightly disturbed.

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:55.140
That's the types of things
we can see with Sentinel-1.

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:58.770
Then you have clay
minerals ratio.

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:00.310
And then finally,
on the far right,

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:05.070
we have high resolution human
visible red, green, and blue

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:06.050
spectrums of light.

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:09.150
So that's things we
would use for our more

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:12.810
kind of computer vision
or TensorFlow type models.

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:16.230
That would be things like
maybe detecting oil pads,

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:17.310
in this case.

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:25.270
So we work all over the world.

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:27.190
We build crop models
all over the world

00:09:27.190 --> 00:09:29.920
for all different
types of crops.

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:33.490
In this talk, I can only kind of
go into a little bit of detail

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:36.790
about one, and I'll
talk about corn.

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:40.900
And you might say, well, what's
this machine learning model of?

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:42.880
Well, I think the only
thing this could detect

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:47.200
is the state of Iowa and
maybe the city of Des Moines.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:50.680
But it probably isn't useful
for classifying much else really

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:52.790
in this example.

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:56.950
But we have a lot of really
complicated processing steps

00:09:56.950 --> 00:09:58.960
to get to that point.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:02.980
We have to be able to
identify and segment out

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:04.520
all the different fields.

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:07.450
We have to know what's
like an urban area

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:10.480
because we don't want to
include those geographic areas

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:11.990
into our models.

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:14.680
Then we finally have to
run a classifier to say

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:17.620
is this a cornfield, a wheat
field, a soybean field,

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:18.670
those types of things.

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:24.940
And so then after we had
those types of information,

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:28.690
then we can finally start
to estimate how much food

00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:32.240
is being produced in every
single one of those fields

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:36.850
and roll that up into various--
the state level, the county

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:41.710
level, and national level to be
able to model the overall food

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:45.490
supply and how this
year's crop production is

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:49.520
going to come onto the market.

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:53.920
And then we'll back test
this for 10 or 15 years,

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:57.460
seeing every single farm
everywhere in the US every day

00:10:57.460 --> 00:11:01.390
and then saying how did that
model do in that drought year

00:11:01.390 --> 00:11:04.620
or in that year we
had flooding in Iowa.

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:07.510
And test the model at day 200.

00:11:07.510 --> 00:11:08.860
How did it do?

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:10.990
So and then we'll
constantly iterate

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:15.160
on that to try and make those
models as accurate as we can.

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:17.500
So that gives us
an idea how much

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:22.190
food is being produced at
what parts of the country.

00:11:22.190 --> 00:11:24.850
But one of the next
things is often

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.610
that's not where it's
getting delivered to.

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:31.010
It's going to somewhere
else in the world.

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:33.400
So problems we
have to think about

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:36.510
are what about rail capacity?

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:39.670
How do you get from the
field or the elevator

00:11:39.670 --> 00:11:42.760
to the river where
you would have barges?

00:11:42.760 --> 00:11:45.880
And those barges would
take these commodities

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:48.370
down the river.

00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:50.470
Then there's traffic
and traffic delays

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:55.260
on places like the Mississippi
River, believe it or not.

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:56.490
How busy are they?

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:58.440
Is there delays at the port?

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:01.320
Is weather going to
impact those routes?

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:04.110
Are the delays in places
like the Panama Canal?

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:08.310
Can I reroute and get a
more efficient speed up

00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:12.340
or better fuel efficiency,
those types of things.

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:15.720
So here's on the bottom,
we're showing optical.

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:19.590
So that's the human visible red,
green, blue beautiful pictures

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:20.920
that we talked about.

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:24.210
And on the top, we
see the AIS route,

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:27.270
and that works even
with cloud cover.

00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:32.500
So AIS, let's dive a
little bit more into that.

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:33.250
What is it?

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:35.080
It's automatic
identification system.

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:38.710
So this is the message
that ships are sending out

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:40.040
to avoid collisions.

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:43.390
It's sending their unique
ID, where their position,

00:12:43.390 --> 00:12:45.340
where the course is.

00:12:45.340 --> 00:12:46.360
And they send kind of--

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:51.400
it's a ping message at given
intervals to tell each other

00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:54.460
and track so they
don't all collide.

00:12:54.460 --> 00:12:59.770
And this data set, like almost
any type of sensor data,

00:12:59.770 --> 00:13:03.310
we're seeing this just
explode dramatically.

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:07.540
A single AIS vendor from
just Descartes Labs,

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:12.280
just one vendor we use captures
tens of billions of AIS pings

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:13.390
a day.

00:13:13.390 --> 00:13:15.060
And so what's
happening is we are

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:17.200
having this explosion in space.

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:22.660
And as satellites go from
these research devices built

00:13:22.660 --> 00:13:25.870
by NASA that cost a half
billion dollars to things

00:13:25.870 --> 00:13:28.130
that grad departments are
building for a few thousand

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:31.000
dollars and they're getting
cheaper to put into space,

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:33.680
that global coverage is
getting quicker and quicker.

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:35.740
So you're just seeing
this infinite growth.

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:38.740
So that's where
having a no ops--

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:42.130
I don't have to add disks, or
capacity, or processing cores

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:43.870
to be able to ingest
that data starts

00:13:43.870 --> 00:13:45.428
to become really valuable.

00:13:45.428 --> 00:13:47.470
So here's a global
visualization just to show you

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:48.910
the traffic patterns.

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:56.970
And about 90% of global trade is
actually done over the oceans.

00:13:56.970 --> 00:14:01.560
So as you recall, we are
showing corn yields in Iowa,

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:07.760
where the next thing is being
able to model and predict

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:11.840
how delays or impacts on
those barges going down

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:16.190
the waterways in the US, where
those delays are happening

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:18.270
and how things are progressing.

00:14:18.270 --> 00:14:20.290
It's not that
different than if there

00:14:20.290 --> 00:14:22.400
was a delay getting off
the Bay Bridge on your way

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:24.180
here this morning.

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:27.510
But this is for shipping.

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:31.560
And over the past few months
or over just a few months,

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:33.660
we queried--

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:37.050
I think this was a query over
four months on the Mississippi

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:38.490
River.

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:42.000
This is showing we were
able to query down and see

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:46.500
that there was a little
over 350,000 ping messages

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:48.840
for ships.

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:52.020
On a like larger
scale, OK, what's

00:14:52.020 --> 00:14:55.570
happening when they get down to
the bottom of the Mississippi?

00:14:55.570 --> 00:15:00.210
So this is looking at ships
that were in New Orleans

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:04.980
and also in the Panama Canal
and where were they going.

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:09.570
And so this is what used to
be an incredibly hard query

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:12.390
to actually execute, and
it would be really hard

00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:18.400
if we were doing this in
other types of databases.

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:24.110
Because it's just an
incredibly long running query.

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:27.290
So finally, then what
happens when we start

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:29.060
to get into the Panama Canal?

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:34.040
So in this scene, we're showing
synthetic aperture radar.

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:37.190
And you're able to see the
ships starting to line up

00:15:37.190 --> 00:15:39.230
outside of the Panama Canal.

00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:41.480
And that's sending
that radar signal down,

00:15:41.480 --> 00:15:44.200
and you'll be able
to see the difference

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:45.200
in the purple and green.

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:48.800
And you see the ships out there
and how that signal reflects

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:50.200
off the water.

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:53.270
And but again, remember
the problem here

00:15:53.270 --> 00:15:56.630
is we're only passing over
this spot in the Earth

00:15:56.630 --> 00:15:57.440
every few days.

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:01.160
So it's not very high
temporal cadence.

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:02.790
So we need something better.

00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:05.270
So again, that's where
we go back to things

00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:10.610
like being able put this data
into AIS and being able to--

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:14.295
this AIS data set
into a queriable form.

00:16:14.295 --> 00:16:15.920
And then we can start
to see the Panama

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:19.790
Canal in much closer to real
time with many, many data

00:16:19.790 --> 00:16:20.810
points.

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:24.590
And you can see ships
waiting on both the Atlantic

00:16:24.590 --> 00:16:27.450
and the Pacific side.

00:16:27.450 --> 00:16:30.410
To the left colored
in dark is wherever

00:16:30.410 --> 00:16:32.310
ships have really slowed down.

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:35.780
And you can see kind of two
congestion zones in the Panama

00:16:35.780 --> 00:16:39.470
Canal, and then the green we're
seeing where ships are moving

00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:41.720
at a much, much quicker rate.

00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:43.910
So these are the types
of models and insights

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:47.600
we're trying to build for our
customers to really enable

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:51.050
them to make better decisions
on their shipping, and supply

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:58.550
chain, and logistics, and
impact their business.

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:02.980
And so that's great for
grain and grain modeling.

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:06.970
But the next, we're going
to turn it over to David,

00:17:06.970 --> 00:17:10.750
and he's going to
talk about fishing.

00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:12.910
DAVID KROODSMA: Thank you, Tim.

00:17:12.910 --> 00:17:14.329
Hi, my name is David Kroodsma.

00:17:14.329 --> 00:17:16.329
And I am the director of
research and innovation

00:17:16.329 --> 00:17:17.770
at Global Fishing Watch.

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:20.290
And I'm going to share with
you a similar workflow where

00:17:20.290 --> 00:17:24.670
we take radar imagery from
Sentinel-1 across the globe,

00:17:24.670 --> 00:17:27.880
process it using Google Earth
Engine to identify where

00:17:27.880 --> 00:17:30.740
we think there are vessels
all over the globe,

00:17:30.740 --> 00:17:34.630
and then combine that with
AIS data using spatial

00:17:34.630 --> 00:17:38.422
BigQuery to identify
where we think all

00:17:38.422 --> 00:17:40.630
the world's fishing vessels
that are not broadcasting

00:17:40.630 --> 00:17:41.680
their location are.

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:46.330
So I work for Global
Fishing Watch.

00:17:46.330 --> 00:17:50.230
Global Fishing Watch, we're an
independent nonprofit dedicated

00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:53.980
to improving transparency in
the industrial fishing industry.

00:17:53.980 --> 00:17:55.870
We were originally
founded in 2014

00:17:55.870 --> 00:17:58.717
by Google and two nonprofits,
Sky Truth and Google,

00:17:58.717 --> 00:18:00.300
although now we are
our own nonprofit,

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:02.960
and we are entirely
foundation supported.

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:05.020
We have about 30 staff
globally distributed,

00:18:05.020 --> 00:18:08.080
although we do meet
up every now and then.

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:11.590
And we have almost 40,000
registered map users

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:14.050
that go to our website,
globalfishingwatch.org,

00:18:14.050 --> 00:18:17.290
which you can visit, and look at
our interactive map of fishing

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:18.790
activity.

00:18:18.790 --> 00:18:21.820
So our goal is to increase
transparency in the world's

00:18:21.820 --> 00:18:23.140
fishing industry.

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:25.720
And when most of us
think of fishing,

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:28.220
this is what we think of.

00:18:28.220 --> 00:18:30.860
However, this is
not the fishing we

00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:33.230
are trying to track at
Global Fishing Watch.

00:18:33.230 --> 00:18:38.070
We are trying to track fishing
that looks much more like this.

00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:40.400
So this is a picture
from China at the end

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:44.300
of their summer at a port
where fishing vessels

00:18:44.300 --> 00:18:45.800
are leaving the fish.

00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:47.930
And the way China
manages its fisheries

00:18:47.930 --> 00:18:50.270
is that during the summer,
there is a three month--

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:52.190
actually now a four
month moratorium

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:53.690
where no fishing is allowed.

00:18:53.690 --> 00:18:56.060
And at the end of
that, all these vessels

00:18:56.060 --> 00:18:57.540
are allowed to go out and fish.

00:18:57.540 --> 00:19:02.040
So if you're a fish, this is
what's coming out for you.

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:06.000
And you can zoom out and just
see the scale of this fleet.

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:09.030
And China does have the
world's largest fleet,

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:10.830
but this is not just China.

00:19:10.830 --> 00:19:12.660
We are fishing all
the world's oceans

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:15.338
using large industrial
vessels to catch fish.

00:19:15.338 --> 00:19:17.130
And of course, we eat
this fish, but that's

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:18.690
how we're fishing the oceans.

00:19:18.690 --> 00:19:20.482
And what we've done at
Global Fishing Watch

00:19:20.482 --> 00:19:24.060
is zoomed out one more time,
using all of that position data

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:28.320
from AIS to map all of
the fishing activity

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:32.820
by large industrial
vessels that have AIS.

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:34.680
AIS, as Tim
mentioned, is a system

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:37.470
by which vessels broadcast their
location so that other vessels

00:19:37.470 --> 00:19:39.010
can see where they are.

00:19:39.010 --> 00:19:41.040
And it's only required
on large vessels,

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:43.040
so again, we're only
seeing the large ones here,

00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:45.900
but we can track what
their activity is.

00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:48.090
So you may ask, so
why does this matter?

00:19:48.090 --> 00:19:49.890
I mean, it looks
exciting, but why do we

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:53.920
care about transparency
in fisheries?

00:19:53.920 --> 00:19:56.350
Here's why.

00:19:56.350 --> 00:19:57.940
Over a billion
people in the world

00:19:57.940 --> 00:20:02.440
depend on fish for their
primary source of protein.

00:20:02.440 --> 00:20:06.100
Yet, one in third fisheries
are overfished globally.

00:20:06.100 --> 00:20:08.260
And about one in
five fish are caught

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:12.350
through means that are illegal,
unreported, or unregulated.

00:20:12.350 --> 00:20:14.620
So in summary, fish is
critical to the global food

00:20:14.620 --> 00:20:17.690
supply, especially some of the
poorer people on the planet,

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:19.630
yet it has a high
ecological cost.

00:20:19.630 --> 00:20:21.280
We're not doing it sustainably.

00:20:21.280 --> 00:20:23.360
And we're monitoring
it extremely poorly.

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:25.100
And it's also
involved in a lot--

00:20:25.100 --> 00:20:27.580
there's also a lot of crime
and illegal activity associated

00:20:27.580 --> 00:20:28.080
with it.

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:31.390
So our belief is that if we
can track fishing activity more

00:20:31.390 --> 00:20:33.300
efficiently, make
it more transparent,

00:20:33.300 --> 00:20:36.280
we can make it both
more sustainable

00:20:36.280 --> 00:20:37.950
and also provide
food for people.

00:20:41.603 --> 00:20:42.270
How do we do it?

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:45.640
So our key technology is
we use two machine learning

00:20:45.640 --> 00:20:49.898
algorithms, two neural nets,
to identify fishing activity.

00:20:49.898 --> 00:20:51.940
And if you look at-- these
are showing the tracks

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:54.010
of a trawler, which is
a type of vessel that

00:20:54.010 --> 00:20:57.730
drags nets behind it,
a longliner, which

00:20:57.730 --> 00:21:01.940
sets long lines of hooks,
and a purse seiner, which

00:21:01.940 --> 00:21:03.340
circles fish with a net.

00:21:03.340 --> 00:21:05.110
And these are the
tracks from the AIS data

00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:06.410
we just plot how they move.

00:21:06.410 --> 00:21:08.560
And you can see with
your eye how differently

00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:10.160
each of these vessels move.

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:12.310
So we're able to train a
classifier that guesses

00:21:12.310 --> 00:21:15.700
based solely on how the boat
is moving what type of vessel

00:21:15.700 --> 00:21:16.993
it is.

00:21:16.993 --> 00:21:18.910
We also can tell what
are non-fishing vessels,

00:21:18.910 --> 00:21:21.610
so identify what are
the tugboats, what

00:21:21.610 --> 00:21:24.820
are the tankers, what are
the passenger vessels.

00:21:24.820 --> 00:21:26.620
So that we can
ignore those and just

00:21:26.620 --> 00:21:28.420
look at the fishing vessels.

00:21:28.420 --> 00:21:30.040
We then have a second
model, which is,

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:36.080
based on how these boats
move, what is the likelihood

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:37.610
that it's fishing?

00:21:37.610 --> 00:21:40.530
We put those two
together onto a map,

00:21:40.530 --> 00:21:43.040
and we're able to make a
global map of fishing activity

00:21:43.040 --> 00:21:44.340
by these vessels.

00:21:44.340 --> 00:21:47.360
And here it is for
one year of activity.

00:21:47.360 --> 00:21:51.770
And this is probably the biggest
I have ever projected this map,

00:21:51.770 --> 00:21:55.700
although I have looked
at this many, many times.

00:21:55.700 --> 00:21:56.460
I love this map.

00:21:56.460 --> 00:21:57.543
It's beautiful to look at.

00:21:57.543 --> 00:21:58.140
It's exciting.

00:21:58.140 --> 00:21:59.220
You see all types of--

00:21:59.220 --> 00:22:02.130
I see new things every
time I look at it.

00:22:02.130 --> 00:22:03.800
But it's not just a pretty map.

00:22:03.800 --> 00:22:06.500
Creating this global data
set of fishing activity

00:22:06.500 --> 00:22:08.120
has had real impacts.

00:22:08.120 --> 00:22:10.640
In addition to being the first
ever global map of fishing

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:13.070
activity, the data
has been used to help

00:22:13.070 --> 00:22:15.950
identify six places for
marine protected areas, which

00:22:15.950 --> 00:22:18.710
has an area the size of Egypt.

00:22:18.710 --> 00:22:21.320
We've gotten a few countries,
including Indonesia, Peru,

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:24.710
and Panama, with Indonesia
and Peru two of the top five

00:22:24.710 --> 00:22:28.250
fishing countries in the world
to commit to transparency.

00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:29.940
In the last three
years, our data set

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:32.240
and our research
partners have produced

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:34.670
over 20 peer reviewed papers.

00:22:34.670 --> 00:22:37.850
And one of these
papers identified how,

00:22:37.850 --> 00:22:40.700
looking at how boats fish,
identified how subsidies

00:22:40.700 --> 00:22:43.940
are helping drive overfishing.

00:22:43.940 --> 00:22:46.790
And so that has led to an
engagement with the World Trade

00:22:46.790 --> 00:22:50.450
Organization, where our data
is being used in negotiations

00:22:50.450 --> 00:22:52.520
to identify how we
can change $20 billion

00:22:52.520 --> 00:22:54.830
in fishery subsidies,
a lot of which

00:22:54.830 --> 00:22:58.430
drive and promote overfishing.

00:22:58.430 --> 00:22:59.690
So this is really exciting.

00:22:59.690 --> 00:23:02.655
It's really great what
we've been able to do.

00:23:02.655 --> 00:23:04.030
But there's one
problem, which is

00:23:04.030 --> 00:23:06.670
that not all the boats
broadcast their location

00:23:06.670 --> 00:23:10.060
and also some of them don't
broadcast the right location.

00:23:10.060 --> 00:23:12.520
So, so far, we have
tracked the positions

00:23:12.520 --> 00:23:14.890
of all vessels voluntarily
broadcasting their positions.

00:23:14.890 --> 00:23:17.860
What about vessels that
tamper with their AIS device

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:20.110
or broadcast wrong positions?

00:23:20.110 --> 00:23:21.790
So as sort of a
reminder, the way

00:23:21.790 --> 00:23:23.230
we're telling where
these boats go

00:23:23.230 --> 00:23:27.190
is that there is a GPS device on
these vessels with a radio that

00:23:27.190 --> 00:23:30.170
then broadcasts the latitude,
longitude, timestamp

00:23:30.170 --> 00:23:33.410
and a few other characteristics
to other boats nearby

00:23:33.410 --> 00:23:35.080
and then also to satellites.

00:23:35.080 --> 00:23:38.380
Some vessels have changed
the latitude and longitude

00:23:38.380 --> 00:23:41.870
that they're broadcasting so
it says they're somewhere else.

00:23:41.870 --> 00:23:43.870
We're not sure why some
of them are doing this,

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:45.370
but some of them
are, and the result

00:23:45.370 --> 00:23:47.980
is you get wrong
positions in the data.

00:23:47.980 --> 00:23:49.330
Here's a great example.

00:23:49.330 --> 00:23:51.160
Down there on the
bottom left of this,

00:23:51.160 --> 00:23:53.560
you see a boat says it's
in the Southern Pacific.

00:23:53.560 --> 00:23:56.860
Then it says it goes south
through Antarctica, then goes

00:23:56.860 --> 00:24:01.210
to negative 95 degrees
south, which is impossible,

00:24:01.210 --> 00:24:03.400
and then back up.

00:24:03.400 --> 00:24:05.440
It's not there.

00:24:05.440 --> 00:24:08.320
It is actually up there
close to the coast of Peru

00:24:08.320 --> 00:24:10.620
going around South America.

00:24:10.620 --> 00:24:14.490
And we need some automatic
way to identify this.

00:24:14.490 --> 00:24:17.520
And one thing we do know is
we know when each message is

00:24:17.520 --> 00:24:20.550
received by a satellite, and
we know where the satellite is

00:24:20.550 --> 00:24:22.583
when it receives that message.

00:24:22.583 --> 00:24:24.000
And so we can look
at the position

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:26.190
of the satellite,
which usually has

00:24:26.190 --> 00:24:31.230
to be within about 3,000
kilometers of the boat

00:24:31.230 --> 00:24:33.990
to receive a message, to say
roughly where in the world

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:34.770
it is.

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:36.990
But it turns out you get a much
more accurate estimate of where

00:24:36.990 --> 00:24:39.480
that boat is if you take the
average position of satellites

00:24:39.480 --> 00:24:41.605
that receive the messages
at the times they receive

00:24:41.605 --> 00:24:42.813
the message over a given day.

00:24:42.813 --> 00:24:44.230
So what you want
to do is you want

00:24:44.230 --> 00:24:45.855
to take the average
position of lat

00:24:45.855 --> 00:24:48.390
and long over a day of a
vessel and then the average lat

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:50.307
and long of the satellite
positions over a day

00:24:50.307 --> 00:24:51.580
and compare them.

00:24:51.580 --> 00:24:55.740
So you need to
average lat and long.

00:24:55.740 --> 00:24:58.290
That's actually very
tricky on a sphere.

00:24:58.290 --> 00:25:01.260
And before spatial BigQuery,
we did something like this.

00:25:01.260 --> 00:25:03.550
Oh yeah, so the vessel's
actually up there.

00:25:03.550 --> 00:25:06.090
Before spatial
BigQuery, did something

00:25:06.090 --> 00:25:08.280
like this, which is a lot
of trigonometry to turn

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:11.250
the latitude and longitude
into x, y, and z,

00:25:11.250 --> 00:25:12.810
and then average
the x, y, and z,

00:25:12.810 --> 00:25:15.410
and then turn them back
into latitude and longitude.

00:25:15.410 --> 00:25:19.040
And it took 32 lines of SQL.

00:25:19.040 --> 00:25:24.100
And with spatial BigQuery,
we are now able to do this.

00:25:24.100 --> 00:25:26.960
And just the key calculation
is just one line,

00:25:26.960 --> 00:25:30.570
where we turn st_centroid_agg
from the latitude and longitude

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:33.140
of all the points
for a given day

00:25:33.140 --> 00:25:35.750
and then are able to get where
that average location is.

00:25:38.510 --> 00:25:40.820
So the one thing I
failed to say earlier

00:25:40.820 --> 00:25:43.760
is that all of our AIS
data is in BigQuery.

00:25:43.760 --> 00:25:47.630
And we use it every day to use
to do almost every analysis

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:48.650
on what boats are doing.

00:25:48.650 --> 00:25:52.340
And it has been
incredibly useful

00:25:52.340 --> 00:25:53.990
in every way of analysis.

00:25:53.990 --> 00:25:57.713
And now, we started using
it before spatial functions.

00:25:57.713 --> 00:25:59.630
And there's tons of cases
where we figured out

00:25:59.630 --> 00:26:01.047
a really hard way
to do something,

00:26:01.047 --> 00:26:03.350
and it turns out this
spatial BigQuery can now

00:26:03.350 --> 00:26:05.330
save us tons of time
and effort and also

00:26:05.330 --> 00:26:06.650
allow us to do things
we weren't able to do.

00:26:06.650 --> 00:26:08.108
Simple things like
just calculating

00:26:08.108 --> 00:26:11.750
the distance between points
is so much easier now.

00:26:11.750 --> 00:26:12.690
So here's one example.

00:26:12.690 --> 00:26:14.960
So we can identify these
vessels more easily

00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:17.070
that have their offset
in their position.

00:26:17.070 --> 00:26:19.660
But there's a bigger
problem, which

00:26:19.660 --> 00:26:23.980
is that most fishing vessels
don't broadcast their AIS.

00:26:23.980 --> 00:26:26.110
We call this the dark fleet.

00:26:26.110 --> 00:26:28.480
And a lot of them aren't
regulated to carry it,

00:26:28.480 --> 00:26:29.780
so they don't have to.

00:26:29.780 --> 00:26:31.630
But you can also
imagine that if you

00:26:31.630 --> 00:26:34.030
want to engage in
an illegal activity,

00:26:34.030 --> 00:26:36.520
you are most likely
not to broadcast AIS.

00:26:36.520 --> 00:26:38.980
So we want to see these boats.

00:26:38.980 --> 00:26:42.160
How can we do it?

00:26:42.160 --> 00:26:44.650
Well, one thing we
can do is use radar.

00:26:44.650 --> 00:26:48.160
So as Tim talked about, there's
a satellite called Sentinel-1

00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:51.340
from the European Space
Agency that has radar.

00:26:51.340 --> 00:26:55.330
And the radar can penetrate
clouds and see bright objects

00:26:55.330 --> 00:26:55.970
on water.

00:26:55.970 --> 00:26:58.645
So this is a composite
image over several months.

00:26:58.645 --> 00:27:00.770
If you look between the
United Kingdom and Europe--

00:27:00.770 --> 00:27:01.340
that's what you're
looking there,

00:27:01.340 --> 00:27:03.850
at the English Channel--
you can see all those dots.

00:27:03.850 --> 00:27:05.307
That's the shipping lanes.

00:27:05.307 --> 00:27:07.390
And that's because metal
objects on water show up.

00:27:07.390 --> 00:27:10.070
It's really bright in radar.

00:27:10.070 --> 00:27:12.440
And what we can do is we
can look at these scenes,

00:27:12.440 --> 00:27:16.540
and we can count where
there are likely vessels.

00:27:16.540 --> 00:27:18.860
Sentinel-1 is the
satellite we're using,

00:27:18.860 --> 00:27:21.530
and it covers all the
world's terrestrial service.

00:27:21.530 --> 00:27:23.210
It doesn't do the
oceans, but it does

00:27:23.210 --> 00:27:25.160
do all the world's coastlines.

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:26.993
And most vessels
are close to shore.

00:27:26.993 --> 00:27:28.660
So what we've done
is we said, OK, we're

00:27:28.660 --> 00:27:30.800
going to process all
of the Sentinel-1 that

00:27:30.800 --> 00:27:32.870
overlaps with the coastlines.

00:27:32.870 --> 00:27:38.710
That's a few 100,000 images
and over a petabyte of data.

00:27:38.710 --> 00:27:41.020
How do we process a
petabyte of radar imagery?

00:27:41.020 --> 00:27:43.240
As I said, we're
a small nonprofit.

00:27:43.240 --> 00:27:45.490
We don't have tons of
servers sitting around.

00:27:45.490 --> 00:27:48.573
But we do have
Google Earth Engine.

00:27:48.573 --> 00:27:50.240
And Google Earth
Engine has two features

00:27:50.240 --> 00:27:52.177
that make it extremely
useful for us.

00:27:52.177 --> 00:27:54.260
The first is that they
have-- it's a data catalog.

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:56.360
And so they've organized
satellite imagery

00:27:56.360 --> 00:28:00.290
from tons of the most useful
freely available satellite

00:28:00.290 --> 00:28:02.600
archives, including, say,
the entire-- and they have

00:28:02.600 --> 00:28:05.450
the entire Sentinel-1 archive
just sitting there in servers

00:28:05.450 --> 00:28:08.083
so that we don't need to
download and pre-process it.

00:28:08.083 --> 00:28:09.500
Then built on top
of that, there's

00:28:09.500 --> 00:28:11.600
a computational
platform that allows

00:28:11.600 --> 00:28:14.870
you to do analyses
at scale distributed

00:28:14.870 --> 00:28:19.200
across all of these images.

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:22.790
So we can figure out a process
for one image from Sentinel-1

00:28:22.790 --> 00:28:25.190
and then hit Run and run
it across the whole globe.

00:28:27.942 --> 00:28:30.150
Here's a picture of what
the viewer of it looks like.

00:28:30.150 --> 00:28:32.090
So there's a nice JavaScript.

00:28:32.090 --> 00:28:36.910
There's a nice web based
JavaScript editor here

00:28:36.910 --> 00:28:37.410
at the top.

00:28:37.410 --> 00:28:40.000
You can see you can edit
your code and share it.

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:44.060
It has an interactive map that
you can look at and work on.

00:28:44.060 --> 00:28:45.560
And so you can kind
of play with it

00:28:45.560 --> 00:28:49.280
and see what images look like
as you're analyzing them.

00:28:49.280 --> 00:28:51.590
And then run.

00:28:51.590 --> 00:28:54.355
There's also a Python API.

00:28:54.355 --> 00:28:55.730
And so we use the
JavaScript tool

00:28:55.730 --> 00:28:57.980
to kind of do the
inspections, write the code,

00:28:57.980 --> 00:29:01.700
and then we move it over
and use the Python API when

00:29:01.700 --> 00:29:06.155
we want to put it
into production to run

00:29:06.155 --> 00:29:08.030
across the whole globe,
although you can also

00:29:08.030 --> 00:29:10.730
do some of that in the
JavaScript console.

00:29:13.300 --> 00:29:16.680
So let's look at one of
these Sentinel-1 scenes.

00:29:16.680 --> 00:29:17.730
Let's zoom in.

00:29:17.730 --> 00:29:19.410
So this is what it looks like.

00:29:19.410 --> 00:29:21.720
The one you're looking on
the left there is Italy.

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:22.890
On the right is Croatia.

00:29:22.890 --> 00:29:24.180
This is in the Adriatic Sea.

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:27.480
It's about 200 miles
across, is one scene.

00:29:27.480 --> 00:29:30.600
The dark area there is
the footprint of the scene

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:32.010
that we have analyzed.

00:29:32.010 --> 00:29:35.468
And all of those dots
are where our algorithm--

00:29:35.468 --> 00:29:37.260
that we use in Earth
Engine is just looking

00:29:37.260 --> 00:29:38.700
for bright spots on the water--

00:29:38.700 --> 00:29:42.370
says that there are
probably vessels on vessels.

00:29:42.370 --> 00:29:45.360
Now we want to compare this
with the AIS data, which is also

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:47.497
in BigQuery, to say
which of these dots

00:29:47.497 --> 00:29:49.830
are fishing boats, which of
these are not fishing boats,

00:29:49.830 --> 00:29:53.400
and which of these are
vessels that do not have AIS.

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:55.167
To do that's a
little complicated

00:29:55.167 --> 00:29:57.000
because you can imagine
the satellite images

00:29:57.000 --> 00:29:58.770
taken at one time.

00:29:58.770 --> 00:30:00.937
And then your AIS
positions aren't always

00:30:00.937 --> 00:30:02.520
exactly at the point
of the satellite.

00:30:02.520 --> 00:30:03.930
They may be a little bit before.

00:30:03.930 --> 00:30:05.530
They may be a little bit after.

00:30:05.530 --> 00:30:08.520
And you need to do some smart
interpolation between those two

00:30:08.520 --> 00:30:10.860
points to get the most
likely position of the vessel

00:30:10.860 --> 00:30:15.000
at the time of the satellite to
match it to these detections.

00:30:15.000 --> 00:30:17.250
Also, you want to clip
it to just this scene

00:30:17.250 --> 00:30:19.500
so you compare it to only
the scene you're looking at.

00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:22.890
And I don't have to process and
compare all the points outside.

00:30:22.890 --> 00:30:26.100
So what we've done is we've
loaded all of this information

00:30:26.100 --> 00:30:28.872
into BigQuery from the
output of Earth Engine.

00:30:28.872 --> 00:30:31.080
So we take the output of
Earth Engine, which gives us

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:34.140
all of these positions
messages, and then also we

00:30:34.140 --> 00:30:37.440
take the outline of the
footprint, so a polygon

00:30:37.440 --> 00:30:39.120
of the footprint of that image.

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:41.970
And we upload the
footprint, that

00:30:41.970 --> 00:30:44.460
footprint as a well-known
text into BigQuery,

00:30:44.460 --> 00:30:46.770
as well as all these detections.

00:30:46.770 --> 00:30:49.920
And then we can do a
JOIN on the AIS data.

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:53.910
So we interpolate
the AIS data and then

00:30:53.910 --> 00:30:56.700
do an ST contains
for points that

00:30:56.700 --> 00:30:59.790
are within the footprint of the
satellite at the right position

00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:03.990
to identify which vessels
are most likely to match up

00:31:03.990 --> 00:31:06.540
with which detections
from radar.

00:31:06.540 --> 00:31:09.600
And in this scene, lots
of vessels use AIS.

00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:12.317
So we can match it quite well.

00:31:12.317 --> 00:31:14.400
And so the red dots there
are things that we think

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:15.680
are fishing vessels.

00:31:15.680 --> 00:31:18.570
The turquoise ones are ones
that are non-fishing vessels.

00:31:18.570 --> 00:31:21.230
And the gray ones are ones
that were not matched.

00:31:21.230 --> 00:31:22.830
They could be
objects in the water.

00:31:22.830 --> 00:31:24.787
We're identifying
those another way.

00:31:24.787 --> 00:31:27.120
But a lot of them are vessels
that do not broadcast AIS.

00:31:29.890 --> 00:31:31.570
Zoom out to the
entire Adriatic Sea.

00:31:31.570 --> 00:31:33.460
You can see Italy's
on the left there.

00:31:33.460 --> 00:31:36.550
And to the right is the
former Yugoslavian countries.

00:31:36.550 --> 00:31:38.500
And then in the south is Greece.

00:31:38.500 --> 00:31:42.800
And this is all the detections
over one year in this area.

00:31:42.800 --> 00:31:44.380
And you can see
the shipping lanes.

00:31:44.380 --> 00:31:46.180
You can see some
fishing activity.

00:31:46.180 --> 00:31:49.180
And we now are able to
put all this into BigQuery

00:31:49.180 --> 00:31:52.390
and match it at scale to
identify which of these we

00:31:52.390 --> 00:31:57.260
think are non-fishing vessels,
which of these are fishing

00:31:57.260 --> 00:32:01.170
vessels, and which of
these are not matched.

00:32:01.170 --> 00:32:04.250
If you look on the
right on the east coast

00:32:04.250 --> 00:32:08.930
there, on the east side of the
sea close to what is Albania,

00:32:08.930 --> 00:32:11.030
you'll see a bunch
of gray activity.

00:32:11.030 --> 00:32:14.210
Albania does not require
its fleet to carry AIS.

00:32:14.210 --> 00:32:16.182
So that could be
the fishing fleet.

00:32:16.182 --> 00:32:17.640
And we've done one
more thing here,

00:32:17.640 --> 00:32:20.060
which is we have trained a
model to identify based solely

00:32:20.060 --> 00:32:22.730
on where an object
is in the ocean,

00:32:22.730 --> 00:32:26.300
using features such as
the bathymetry, the slope

00:32:26.300 --> 00:32:28.130
of the bathymetry,
whether or not

00:32:28.130 --> 00:32:31.490
it's close to a shipping
lane, the distance from shore.

00:32:31.490 --> 00:32:34.000
All these features which we
actually generated with JOINs

00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:36.885
in BigQuery, we use those
features on each observation

00:32:36.885 --> 00:32:39.260
and are able to make a guess
about whether it's a fishing

00:32:39.260 --> 00:32:42.080
boat or a non-fishing vessel.

00:32:42.080 --> 00:32:44.480
I've also added in the bottom
here a dotted line here,

00:32:44.480 --> 00:32:46.610
that yellow dotted
line, which is

00:32:46.610 --> 00:32:50.850
the area of a proposed
marine protected area.

00:32:50.850 --> 00:32:51.500
Sorry.

00:32:51.500 --> 00:32:54.440
So we trained a model,
and we're able to guess

00:32:54.440 --> 00:32:57.260
with some confidence,
some probabilistically,

00:32:57.260 --> 00:33:00.450
that those red dots are
likely fishing vessels.

00:33:00.450 --> 00:33:03.200
So you can see that's a dark
fleet of fishing vessels

00:33:03.200 --> 00:33:06.650
there on the right side
that are not broadcasting.

00:33:06.650 --> 00:33:08.010
Why does this matter?

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:09.800
Well, the yellow
dotted line there

00:33:09.800 --> 00:33:11.660
is a marine
protected area that's

00:33:11.660 --> 00:33:13.880
been proposed in the
southern Adriatic.

00:33:13.880 --> 00:33:18.350
And so we had a nonprofit in
Europe come to us and say,

00:33:18.350 --> 00:33:20.660
can you tell us what fishing
activity is happening

00:33:20.660 --> 00:33:21.950
within this proposed area?

00:33:21.950 --> 00:33:24.200
Because we want to talk
with the fishing industry

00:33:24.200 --> 00:33:25.880
and have negotiations
about where

00:33:25.880 --> 00:33:28.340
this marine protected area
will be, how much fishing

00:33:28.340 --> 00:33:30.240
will be affected by it.

00:33:30.240 --> 00:33:32.180
And we don't know how
much fishing is there.

00:33:32.180 --> 00:33:34.640
So we're able to use
this method to then--

00:33:34.640 --> 00:33:36.270
now let's zoom into that area.

00:33:36.270 --> 00:33:39.557
And there's a bunch of fishing
on the west side on the left

00:33:39.557 --> 00:33:42.140
that you can't see because it's
underneath the turquoise dots.

00:33:42.140 --> 00:33:44.210
And let's just look at
the fishing and zoom in.

00:33:44.210 --> 00:33:48.680
And we're able to say that is
the fishing activity over 2017

00:33:48.680 --> 00:33:52.910
of vessels within that proposed
area, which is now extremely

00:33:52.910 --> 00:33:56.900
useful for having those
discussions about where to site

00:33:56.900 --> 00:33:58.040
this marine protected area.

00:34:01.520 --> 00:34:05.560
So again, we're trying to create
transparency in the world's

00:34:05.560 --> 00:34:07.000
fishing industry.

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:09.010
Being able to use
spatial BigQuery

00:34:09.010 --> 00:34:11.710
has been a huge assist
to us because we

00:34:11.710 --> 00:34:13.989
spend so much of our time
in BigQuery analyzing

00:34:13.989 --> 00:34:15.550
data, processing things.

00:34:15.550 --> 00:34:17.800
And these functions have
allowed us to really get

00:34:17.800 --> 00:34:20.020
another few steps forward
in creating transparency

00:34:20.020 --> 00:34:21.100
in fishing.

00:34:21.100 --> 00:34:23.770
And the reason we want to
create transparency in fishing

00:34:23.770 --> 00:34:26.350
is because we want
more places like this.

00:34:26.350 --> 00:34:30.280
This is a real picture taken by
one of our research partners'

00:34:30.280 --> 00:34:34.150
teams, Enric Sala of National
Geographic Pristine Seas, that

00:34:34.150 --> 00:34:36.880
shows off a marine
protected area that

00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:38.860
has an incredible
abundance of sharks.

00:34:38.860 --> 00:34:41.239
And our goal is to protect--

00:34:41.239 --> 00:34:44.114
have as many places like
this as we can in the world.

00:34:44.114 --> 00:34:46.239
And one of the ways we can
do that is to illuminate

00:34:46.239 --> 00:34:47.980
the world's dark
fleets, which we think

00:34:47.980 --> 00:34:51.699
will help end illegal,
unreported, unregulated fishing

00:34:51.699 --> 00:34:53.409
by industrial fishing vessels.

00:34:55.977 --> 00:34:56.810
Thank you very much.

00:34:56.810 --> 00:34:58.880
And now I'm going to
hand it off to Mosha, who

00:34:58.880 --> 00:35:01.763
is the chief BigQuery engineer.

00:35:01.763 --> 00:35:03.680
I do have to say, I've
been doing BigQuery now

00:35:03.680 --> 00:35:04.968
for the last 3 and 1/2 years.

00:35:04.968 --> 00:35:06.635
And I did take a
selfie with him earlier

00:35:06.635 --> 00:35:08.960
because I was very
excited to meet

00:35:08.960 --> 00:35:13.615
the man behind what we've been
using at Global Fishing Watch.

00:35:13.615 --> 00:35:16.730
MOSHA PASUMANSKY: All
right, thank you, David.

00:35:16.730 --> 00:35:19.350
So it's been really
fascinating for me

00:35:19.350 --> 00:35:22.280
to listen to both of those
talks from Tim and David

00:35:22.280 --> 00:35:24.650
and hear how things
that we built

00:35:24.650 --> 00:35:26.570
into product and
to BigQuery, how

00:35:26.570 --> 00:35:30.320
they're used in the real
world for such great causes.

00:35:30.320 --> 00:35:33.980
So my portion of the work
is going to be about--

00:35:33.980 --> 00:35:40.250
I will tell you how BigQuery
was able to achieve all those--

00:35:40.250 --> 00:35:42.080
to be able to power
those applications

00:35:42.080 --> 00:35:46.070
and to be able to do those
special analysis at such

00:35:46.070 --> 00:35:47.030
a big scale.

00:35:47.030 --> 00:35:49.580
So I'm going to talk
about every single detail

00:35:49.580 --> 00:35:52.230
and every single algorithm--

00:35:52.230 --> 00:35:54.650
well, maybe-- maybe
not every single one,

00:35:54.650 --> 00:35:57.890
but hopefully the
most important ones--

00:35:57.890 --> 00:36:00.010
and explain how it works.

00:36:00.010 --> 00:36:03.770
So one little warning-- there
will be a little bit of math

00:36:03.770 --> 00:36:06.470
in this presentation,
but nothing too hard,

00:36:06.470 --> 00:36:10.580
nothing like high
schoolers cannot deal with.

00:36:10.580 --> 00:36:13.730
OK, so let's start.

00:36:13.730 --> 00:36:16.610
One of the great things
about working in Google

00:36:16.610 --> 00:36:21.300
is that we have world expert
on about every single subject.

00:36:21.300 --> 00:36:24.600
And of course, Google has
been doing geography-- dealing

00:36:24.600 --> 00:36:27.500
with geography data and
location data for a very, very

00:36:27.500 --> 00:36:28.190
long time.

00:36:28.190 --> 00:36:30.890
We have products like Google
Maps, and Google Earth Engine,

00:36:30.890 --> 00:36:33.080
and Google searches of
this location [INAUDIBLE],,

00:36:33.080 --> 00:36:34.280
and Android.

00:36:34.280 --> 00:36:37.370
And now it's really
easier to find products

00:36:37.370 --> 00:36:39.680
which don't use locations.

00:36:39.680 --> 00:36:43.267
So there is a lot of
expertise inside Google.

00:36:43.267 --> 00:36:45.350
And all those products are
a little bit different.

00:36:45.350 --> 00:36:48.170
But at the heart of all of
them, at the core of them,

00:36:48.170 --> 00:36:50.720
is this amazing
low-level library

00:36:50.720 --> 00:36:54.530
called S2 Geometry
Library, which

00:36:54.530 --> 00:36:58.130
deals with very low-level
details which are also

00:36:58.130 --> 00:37:01.310
picky details, you know,
how you do all the geometry,

00:37:01.310 --> 00:37:04.760
and how you do it on the
surface of the Earth.

00:37:04.760 --> 00:37:07.850
So really what we were
building was standing

00:37:07.850 --> 00:37:09.700
on the shoulders of giants.

00:37:09.700 --> 00:37:14.670
And we're using-- this
technology started in 2005.

00:37:14.670 --> 00:37:16.020
It's 15 years in the making.

00:37:16.020 --> 00:37:19.085
It's very much
robust, battle-tested.

00:37:19.085 --> 00:37:20.460
And by the way,
it's open source,

00:37:20.460 --> 00:37:22.580
so it's not just
Google who uses it.

00:37:22.580 --> 00:37:24.110
There are many other companies.

00:37:24.110 --> 00:37:28.160
Probably the other most
well-known one is Pokemon Go.

00:37:28.160 --> 00:37:30.320
And I don't know if
anybody is still playing.

00:37:30.320 --> 00:37:35.600
But it is something that
we felt really good about.

00:37:35.600 --> 00:37:38.760
And remember when I said, we
have all the world experts?

00:37:38.760 --> 00:37:42.920
So the guy behind this library,
Eric Veach, he's a legend here.

00:37:42.920 --> 00:37:46.130
When he was a PhD student, he
worked on computer graphics

00:37:46.130 --> 00:37:48.870
and his thesis was
on ray tracing.

00:37:48.870 --> 00:37:51.990
And that algorithm is still
used in pretty much every ray

00:37:51.990 --> 00:37:53.720
tracing software today.

00:37:53.720 --> 00:37:55.520
He actually got Oscars for it.

00:37:55.520 --> 00:37:58.340
If you watched
"Monsters, Incorporated,"

00:37:58.340 --> 00:37:59.770
so that's his work.

00:37:59.770 --> 00:38:02.600
Anyway, he just
happens to work out

00:38:02.600 --> 00:38:05.830
of Seattle, same building,
same floor as BigQuery team is.

00:38:05.830 --> 00:38:07.645
So when we worked
on BigQuery GIS

00:38:07.645 --> 00:38:09.530
and we had some
tricky question, all

00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:13.070
I had to do is stand up,
walk 20 meters, say, Eric,

00:38:13.070 --> 00:38:16.745
how would you deal with
polygons, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:38:16.745 --> 00:38:19.500
And he goes, oh, just
do this and that.

00:38:19.500 --> 00:38:24.680
OK, so we will be going now
into the internals of S2.

00:38:24.680 --> 00:38:27.270
And stay with me.

00:38:27.270 --> 00:38:29.710
I'll explain later how we
use it inside BigQuery,

00:38:29.710 --> 00:38:32.070
and why is it useful
and why is it good.

00:38:32.070 --> 00:38:35.700
OK, so we'll start with
our first assumption

00:38:35.700 --> 00:38:37.530
that Earth is round.

00:38:37.530 --> 00:38:39.270
And we're all grown-ups here.

00:38:39.270 --> 00:38:42.720
We of course that
this is not true.

00:38:42.720 --> 00:38:45.630
And I don't mean that
the world is flat,

00:38:45.630 --> 00:38:48.900
but it's not actually
a sphere, right?

00:38:48.900 --> 00:38:51.220
It's an ellipsoid.

00:38:51.220 --> 00:38:55.330
But you know, a sphere
is easier to work with.

00:38:55.330 --> 00:38:57.750
And I think that all of
the biological properties

00:38:57.750 --> 00:39:00.480
of ellipsoid are
preserved from a sphere.

00:39:00.480 --> 00:39:02.110
Only distances become different.

00:39:02.110 --> 00:39:04.480
So we can compensate
for it later.

00:39:04.480 --> 00:39:07.350
OK, so let's start
with round Earth.

00:39:07.350 --> 00:39:09.090
But first thing we do--

00:39:09.090 --> 00:39:12.300
it's hard to index on a
sphere, so first thing we do

00:39:12.300 --> 00:39:15.940
is say, well, what if
Earth was actually a cube?

00:39:15.940 --> 00:39:18.140
So we draw a cube
around the Earth,

00:39:18.140 --> 00:39:21.090
and we project every single
point to the faces of cube.

00:39:21.090 --> 00:39:22.690
There are six of them.

00:39:22.690 --> 00:39:24.970
And why is it simpler?

00:39:24.970 --> 00:39:27.470
Because the faces of the
cube are actually flat.

00:39:27.470 --> 00:39:31.330
And on a flat surface, we can
do all the arithmetic and all

00:39:31.330 --> 00:39:34.120
the calculations
much, much easier.

00:39:34.120 --> 00:39:37.465
OK, so how we actually project
from a sphere to a cube?

00:39:37.465 --> 00:39:39.570
Well, for a single
point, it's very easy.

00:39:39.570 --> 00:39:41.290
The formula is stable.

00:39:41.290 --> 00:39:43.570
But then what
happens is when you

00:39:43.570 --> 00:39:47.920
project an area, if it's
closer to a quarter, right

00:39:47.920 --> 00:39:50.590
in the middle, it
will be smaller.

00:39:50.590 --> 00:39:52.610
If you go north or
south, it becomes bigger.

00:39:52.610 --> 00:39:54.640
And actually, the
distortion becomes

00:39:54.640 --> 00:39:56.110
as big as five times bigger.

00:39:56.110 --> 00:40:00.470
And this is not good if we
want to do precise indexing.

00:40:00.470 --> 00:40:02.260
So we'll have to
do some adjustment.

00:40:02.260 --> 00:40:03.530
Oops.

00:40:03.530 --> 00:40:05.580
Yeah, we'll have to
do some adjustment.

00:40:05.580 --> 00:40:08.820
Again, it becomes a little bit
tricky to do exact adjustments.

00:40:08.820 --> 00:40:11.710
We need to do arc tangents--
and that's by the way,

00:40:11.710 --> 00:40:16.160
the math I was referring to,
that's the end of the math--

00:40:16.160 --> 00:40:17.780
which is very expensive.

00:40:17.780 --> 00:40:22.490
So what S2 came up with, really
a nice quadratic approximation,

00:40:22.490 --> 00:40:26.710
which is almost as precise as
trigonometry and almost as fast

00:40:26.710 --> 00:40:28.170
as a linear one.

00:40:28.170 --> 00:40:32.897
OK, so now we have a method
of projection points and areas

00:40:32.897 --> 00:40:33.980
onto the face of the cube.

00:40:33.980 --> 00:40:36.650
So let's take one of
the faces of the cube.

00:40:36.650 --> 00:40:37.760
There are six of them.

00:40:37.760 --> 00:40:39.990
So this is one.

00:40:39.990 --> 00:40:41.480
And you see-- we
already projected.

00:40:41.480 --> 00:40:43.140
So see how flat it is?

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:44.730
Because it's on a cube.

00:40:44.730 --> 00:40:48.000
So now in order to index it,
we're going to build a tree.

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:49.920
And usually, in
computer science,

00:40:49.920 --> 00:40:53.880
when people talk about trees,
they talk about binary trees.

00:40:53.880 --> 00:40:55.830
But since it's
two-dimensional now,

00:40:55.830 --> 00:40:57.870
we build what's
called a quad tree.

00:40:57.870 --> 00:41:03.800
So we take the whole face and
divide it into four squares,

00:41:03.800 --> 00:41:06.170
and then take each
one of those squares

00:41:06.170 --> 00:41:09.050
and divide it again
into four squares.

00:41:09.050 --> 00:41:11.240
And we do it again
and again and again.

00:41:11.240 --> 00:41:13.530
Actually, we do it 30 times.

00:41:13.530 --> 00:41:16.400
So we have 30
levels in our tree.

00:41:16.400 --> 00:41:19.430
And each one of those
squares is called cube

00:41:19.430 --> 00:41:22.670
so we have 2 to
the power of 30 2

00:41:22.670 --> 00:41:24.920
to the power of 30, which
is 2 to the power of 60

00:41:24.920 --> 00:41:26.250
different cells.

00:41:26.250 --> 00:41:28.370
If you look at the cell
at the lowest level,

00:41:28.370 --> 00:41:33.900
at the level of 30, it's
about one square centimeter.

00:41:33.900 --> 00:41:35.430
So we have six faces.

00:41:35.430 --> 00:41:38.130
It nicely fits into
64-bit integer.

00:41:38.130 --> 00:41:39.360
So just think about it.

00:41:39.360 --> 00:41:41.740
I'm working here on a sim.

00:41:41.740 --> 00:41:44.980
And each of my steps, I'm
having hundreds of the cells,

00:41:44.980 --> 00:41:48.780
and each one of them has unique
integer associated with it.

00:41:48.780 --> 00:41:49.860
I think that's great.

00:41:49.860 --> 00:41:54.880
So we're using those
integers as our indexes.

00:41:54.880 --> 00:41:58.790
Now, to get good
special indexing--

00:41:58.790 --> 00:42:04.710
by the way, I've often talked
about how BigQuery, everything

00:42:04.710 --> 00:42:06.362
is gone and there
are no indexes.

00:42:06.362 --> 00:42:07.820
So this, of course,
simplification.

00:42:07.820 --> 00:42:11.960
Of course behind the scenes,
it uses all kinds of things,

00:42:11.960 --> 00:42:13.772
just not visible to the user.

00:42:13.772 --> 00:42:15.230
Which is a good
thing for the user,

00:42:15.230 --> 00:42:16.938
because you don't have
to worry about it.

00:42:16.938 --> 00:42:19.290
We do it for you.

00:42:19.290 --> 00:42:21.080
So the next thing
we need to do is

00:42:21.080 --> 00:42:24.020
to figure out how to connect
all those cells together, right?

00:42:24.020 --> 00:42:26.160
It's two-dimensional.

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:29.520
And we build what is
called space filling curve.

00:42:29.520 --> 00:42:31.700
And we use Hilbert curve here.

00:42:31.700 --> 00:42:35.780
And nice property
of this curve is,

00:42:35.780 --> 00:42:40.490
points which are close together
in the real world usually--

00:42:40.490 --> 00:42:42.620
not always, but most
of the time end up

00:42:42.620 --> 00:42:45.990
being close to each
other on this graph.

00:42:45.990 --> 00:42:49.497
OK, for example, those two
cells are close to each other.

00:42:49.497 --> 00:42:51.080
They will be right
next to each other.

00:42:51.080 --> 00:42:52.413
Of course, there are exceptions.

00:42:52.413 --> 00:42:54.300
And sorry, this is too far.

00:42:54.300 --> 00:42:56.600
I cannot jump and show you
a couple of points which are

00:42:56.600 --> 00:42:59.570
close in the real world but
end up being far on the Hilbert

00:42:59.570 --> 00:43:00.170
curve.

00:43:00.170 --> 00:43:03.340
But this is relatively rare.

00:43:03.340 --> 00:43:07.450
So to recap, we talked round
Earth, put it into cube,

00:43:07.450 --> 00:43:08.990
project it, put Hilbert curve.

00:43:08.990 --> 00:43:11.680
And you may wonder,
what does this

00:43:11.680 --> 00:43:14.730
have to do anything
with BigQuery and GIS,

00:43:14.730 --> 00:43:17.120
and how am I going to use it?

00:43:17.120 --> 00:43:20.920
So now I will walk you through
a real example how it's used.

00:43:20.920 --> 00:43:23.110
OK, we're looking at a polygon.

00:43:23.110 --> 00:43:25.000
And people from New
York will immediately

00:43:25.000 --> 00:43:26.570
identify this polygon.

00:43:26.570 --> 00:43:31.210
This is of course
Metropolitan Museum of Art.

00:43:31.210 --> 00:43:33.520
So the polygon is a
little bit complex.

00:43:33.520 --> 00:43:36.090
There are like a dozen
edges and whatnot.

00:43:36.090 --> 00:43:38.540
And let's say what
we wanted to do--

00:43:38.540 --> 00:43:40.120
we also have a lot
of points, and we

00:43:40.120 --> 00:43:42.850
want to find out which
ones are inside the museum

00:43:42.850 --> 00:43:44.470
and which ones are outside.

00:43:44.470 --> 00:43:48.610
So to do it exactly is actually
computationally expensive.

00:43:48.610 --> 00:43:51.310
Think if we were to
check it for every point.

00:43:51.310 --> 00:43:55.210
So this is where we
start using our indexes.

00:43:55.210 --> 00:43:59.560
So what we do first is we try
to find a minimal cell out

00:43:59.560 --> 00:44:01.840
of those hierarchical
cells which

00:44:01.840 --> 00:44:04.600
completely cover cell polygon.

00:44:04.600 --> 00:44:06.330
So here's one.

00:44:06.330 --> 00:44:10.440
And it also covers some area
which is not in the museum--

00:44:10.440 --> 00:44:12.720
in fact, more than half of it.

00:44:12.720 --> 00:44:13.870
So we say, OK--

00:44:13.870 --> 00:44:16.020
well, we don't have
to use single integer.

00:44:16.020 --> 00:44:19.150
Either can use--
those are four cells.

00:44:19.150 --> 00:44:20.830
So it becomes a little better.

00:44:20.830 --> 00:44:24.520
We can use eight cells
or we can use 16 cells.

00:44:24.520 --> 00:44:27.120
So what BigQuery
does, every time

00:44:27.120 --> 00:44:29.460
you have geography
object in BigQuery,

00:44:29.460 --> 00:44:33.240
it automatically picks
the most optimal covering

00:44:33.240 --> 00:44:34.740
over those cell
areas and determines

00:44:34.740 --> 00:44:36.810
which one is just an integer.

00:44:36.810 --> 00:44:38.883
And it does it free of charge.

00:44:38.883 --> 00:44:40.300
You're not paying
anything for it.

00:44:40.300 --> 00:44:40.960
We do it for you.

00:44:40.960 --> 00:44:42.543
You are not paying
for storage, you're

00:44:42.543 --> 00:44:44.340
not paying for computations.

00:44:44.340 --> 00:44:46.800
It's all transparent.

00:44:46.800 --> 00:44:49.260
So there is a trade
off, of course.

00:44:49.260 --> 00:44:51.990
We don't want to use
too many indexes,

00:44:51.990 --> 00:44:53.695
but we also don't
want to use too few.

00:44:53.695 --> 00:44:56.070
So it depends on the shape,
it depends on how many points

00:44:56.070 --> 00:44:57.990
you have.

00:44:57.990 --> 00:45:00.450
I want to say we use
machine learning for that,

00:45:00.450 --> 00:45:05.160
but we use algorithms for that.

00:45:05.160 --> 00:45:06.310
So we build it.

00:45:06.310 --> 00:45:10.080
So the next step is, now
you have all those points

00:45:10.080 --> 00:45:12.630
and you need to figure
out which are inside

00:45:12.630 --> 00:45:14.630
and which one's outside.

00:45:14.630 --> 00:45:19.200
And now we have this
very, very fast algorithm.

00:45:19.200 --> 00:45:22.650
Each point has it's own S2 cell.

00:45:22.650 --> 00:45:26.010
And our object has
its own cell covering.

00:45:26.010 --> 00:45:29.910
So all we need to do is to
compare integers to quickly

00:45:29.910 --> 00:45:33.140
figure out which points have
absolutely no chance of being

00:45:33.140 --> 00:45:34.320
inside polygon.

00:45:34.320 --> 00:45:37.230
And if you think about-- and
if you have millions of them,

00:45:37.230 --> 00:45:41.200
we can pull out a lot of
them very, very quickly.

00:45:41.200 --> 00:45:43.440
Now of course, there will
be some false positives.

00:45:43.440 --> 00:45:45.120
Again, I cannot show.

00:45:45.120 --> 00:45:46.790
And plus, it doesn't work here.

00:45:46.790 --> 00:45:49.440
There are a couple of points
which are inside cover,

00:45:49.440 --> 00:45:50.970
outside of museum.

00:45:50.970 --> 00:45:52.470
We'll have to check
them separately.

00:45:52.470 --> 00:45:55.560
But hopefully, this
number is small.

00:45:55.560 --> 00:45:59.590
So BigQuery actually
does much more than that.

00:45:59.590 --> 00:46:03.630
It also computes the covering
of every shard of data.

00:46:03.630 --> 00:46:07.370
And you know, if you have point
somewhere in Seattle or San

00:46:07.370 --> 00:46:11.570
Francisco, obviously we
have no business checking

00:46:11.570 --> 00:46:12.710
if they are in New York.

00:46:12.710 --> 00:46:17.190
So we can very quickly prune
out all of those partitions.

00:46:17.190 --> 00:46:21.980
And again, all of that happens
completely automatically.

00:46:21.980 --> 00:46:24.330
But for those algorithms
to be effective,

00:46:24.330 --> 00:46:27.950
we also want to make sure that
points which are physically

00:46:27.950 --> 00:46:30.890
close together, they also
end up close together

00:46:30.890 --> 00:46:33.960
inside data or BigQuery table.

00:46:33.960 --> 00:46:36.410
And this is where the
Hilbert curve that we

00:46:36.410 --> 00:46:38.660
talked about will be useful.

00:46:38.660 --> 00:46:40.430
So you know how
BigQuery has clustering

00:46:40.430 --> 00:46:44.360
which usually only works
with integers and strings?

00:46:44.360 --> 00:46:48.410
Well, we can also do clustering
by geography-- not today,

00:46:48.410 --> 00:46:50.920
but very soon we
will realize it.

00:46:50.920 --> 00:46:53.060
So I'm pronouncing this.

00:46:53.060 --> 00:46:54.125
Could I do that?

00:46:54.125 --> 00:46:55.400
Oh, nobody from our team.

00:46:55.400 --> 00:46:56.530
OK, it doesn't matter.

00:46:56.530 --> 00:46:58.880
OK, I'm pronouncing
that very soon you

00:46:58.880 --> 00:47:01.700
will be able to take your
table with geography on it

00:47:01.700 --> 00:47:07.153
and just say, cluster bite,
and you are going to get very--

00:47:07.153 --> 00:47:12.380
I'll skip those-- you will
get spatial clustering,

00:47:12.380 --> 00:47:16.090
and your performance
is going to be great.

00:47:16.090 --> 00:47:18.310
And as we saw in those
applications which

00:47:18.310 --> 00:47:20.380
analyzed the entire
world, it's already great.

00:47:20.380 --> 00:47:22.790
It will become even greater.

00:47:22.790 --> 00:47:26.290
OK, so to recap today,
we looked a little bit

00:47:26.290 --> 00:47:28.610
into how BigQuery
internals work,

00:47:28.610 --> 00:47:31.600
and we looked at a couple of
fairly amazing applications

00:47:31.600 --> 00:47:34.160
that people built on top of it.

00:47:34.160 --> 00:47:38.590
And you can find, on those
links, more info about it.

00:47:38.590 --> 00:47:41.810
And we're looking forward to
you building your application.

00:47:41.810 --> 00:47:42.520
Thank you.

00:47:42.520 --> 00:47:44.320
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:44.320 --> 00:47:48.270
[MUSIC PLAYING]

